    13	
    14	## Summary
    15	
    16	Snapback 2 is the from-scratch successor to Snapback, built in `snapback2/`
    17	inside this repository so that Snapback 1's code, corpus, benchmark, and
    18	diaries are a directory away to steal from — and fenced so that none of
    19	Snapback 1's apparatus applies to it. Its design is already written: the data
    20	language is [LLP 0329.014.004](./0329.014.004-bounded-data-programs-v1-design.rfc.md)
    21	(bounded data programs, static certificate, one execution trace), and the loop
    22	is [LLP 0329.016](./0329.016-agentic-dev-loop-from-scratch.rfc.md) (one owner,
    23	`check` under a second, personas, no silent states, publish constantly). Both
    24	are *proposals* until code here runs them (rules/RULES.md: only implemented
    25	specs bind). This document records the founder's decisions of 2026-08-29 that
    26	turned them into a repository, and is the orientation root for every LLP
    27	numbered 1000 and up.
    28	
    29	## Decisions (author, 2026-08-29)
    30	
    31	1. **In-repo, behind a fence.** `snapback2/` is its own Cargo workspace
    32	   (excluded from the root workspace) and its own Bun workspace (outside the
    33	   root `packages/*`). No Snapback 1 gate, inventory, backstop, registry, or
    34	   script applies to it; the pre-push hook selects gates by path and selects
    35	   none here. Snapback 2 LLPs live in the shared `llp/` numbered from 1000 so
    36	   they can cite the 0xxx corpus.
    37	2. **cdcstack at the root, with baselines.** `rules/RULES.md` and
    38	   `rules/NOT-DOING.md` bind the whole repository; Snapback 1's working set
    39	   (59 links) and oversize files are baselined so the caps bite growth only,
    40	   and its numbers may only fall. `scripts/caps.mjs` is the check.
    41	3. **Ibex 2, embedded now, effects only.** `snapback2-effects` depends on the
    42	   `ibex2` crate pinned to expo/ibex `174c94fb7` (a Rust standard library with
    43	   JS bindings over vanilla, pinned Hermes — ibex LLP 0057/0058/0068). The
    44	   default build links no engine; `--features hermes` links it through
    45	   `scripts/hermes`. Queries and mutations never reach it.
    46	4. **Types by inference.** No generated bytes are committed; the IR,
    47	   certificate, and client stubs are a gitignored build cache.
    48	5. **Three client surfaces, in priority order: React web, Expo, Exact 2.**
    49	   All three matter. **2026-09-06 session-delegated amendment:** Exact 2 waits
    50	   until exact Unicode prefix search qualifies on React web and Expo. The user
    51	   delegated implementation decisions in this session; the resulting trade
    52	   brings engine-maintained `wordPrefixes` and the indexed held-row twin onto
    53	   the doing-list and permits one conventional core `build.rs` for shared
    54	   pinned Unicode inputs. The 2026-09-07 LLP 1018.003 item-surface amendment
    55	   sets 84,000 bytes gzip for the same complete local runtime, with an 80,000
    56	   working target and unchanged runtime resource limits.
    57	   This records a current decision, not a historical author ruling. LLP 1001
    58	   §"Unicode search foundation" and LLP 1012 §"Indexed held search" separate
    59	   the implemented foundation from remaining integration and qualification.
    60	6. **From scratch, building up.** The seed is spike S1's crate (5,140 lines,
    61	   4 dependencies plus SQLite, no Ibex) with `engine.rs` split under the
    62	   1,500-line cap; pieces of Snapback 1 are pulled over one at a time, split
    63	   on the way in, never wholesale.
    64	7. **Iteration speed above everything.** Few, fast tests run locally before
    65	   push; no push gated on a battery; the complete sweep runs on a schedule;
    66	   worktrees are named lanes, reused; the loop is measured on every run
    67	   (`snapback2/scripts/measure.mjs` → `snapback2/metrics/loop.jsonl`) with
    68	   budgets in `rules/RULES.md`.
    69	8. **Publish from day one.** `snapback2/scripts/publish.mjs` builds the
    70	   production binary and publishes the platform package plus the `snapback2`
    71	   shim in seconds; the artifact size is a measured number.
    72	9. **The benchmark is Snapback 1's.** `benchmarks/` (Clique M1–M8, the
    73	   protocol in `benchmarks/protocol.md`) runs against published Snapback 2 as
    74	   soon as `init` and `dev` exist. Promotion is the author's call.
    75	
    76	10. **Quarry is the default authoring surface (author, 2026-08-31).**
    77	    Backends are `snapback/*.q`, compiled by the Quarry front end inside
    78	    the compiler (`packages/compiler/src/quarry/`, in memory, no generated
    79	    files). The TypeScript authoring surface was deleted on 2026-09-02
    80	    (LLP 1007 §4b, LLP 1004 amendment). Since LLP 1018.000's direct compiler
    81	    work, Quarry binds to typed IR; TypeScript is the harness language for
    82	    seeds, journeys, and effect bodies only. Ruling and evidence: [LLP 1004](./1004-quarry-default-surface.decision.md),
    83	    on the executed head-to-head of LLP 0329.014.011; the ruling was
    84	    found unimplemented by LLP 1007 §2 on 2026-09-01 and built on
    85	    2026-09-02 (LLP 1004 §"Implementation record").
    86	
