     1	# Snapback 2 — agent command sheet
     2	
     3	Read this, then `rules/RULES.md` and `rules/NOT-DOING.md` at the repo root, then
     4	`llp/1000-snapback2.explainer.md`; the specs are LLP 1001 (engine), 1002 (loop), 1003 (effects), 1012 (local-first v1; Review, binds after the loop bar).
     5	
     6	```
     7	cargo build                          # data plane + binary; no JS engine, no bootstrap
     8	rust_test_threads="${SNAPBACK_RUST_TEST_THREADS:-$(node -e 'process.stdout.write(String(Math.max(8, Math.min(32, require("node:os").availableParallelism()))))' 2>/dev/null || printf '8\n')}"
     9	NEXTEST_TEST_THREADS="$rust_test_threads" cargo nextest run \
    10	  --config-file ../.config/nextest.toml --profile local-proof \
    11	  --workspace --no-fail-fast          # Rust executable tests (~36 s on the measured EPYC host)
    12	cargo test --workspace --doc --no-fail-fast  # workspace doctests omitted by nextest
    13	scripts/test.sh                      # everything blocking (~84 s warm on EPYC; 60 s total budget)
    14	node scripts/measure.mjs --check     # loop numbers → metrics/loop.jsonl; fails on regression
    15	node scripts/publish.mjs --dry-run   # production build WITH the engine + npm pack; drop --dry-run to ship
    16	scripts/hermes test -p snapback2-effects   # the engine proof (Ibex 2 + vanilla Hermes); never blocking
    17	(cd packages/snapback2 && node build.mjs)  # bundle the compiler worker, client, react, test runner
    18	./target/debug/snapback2 doctor      # boot-to-ready proof: certifies programs, finds the compiler worker
    19	```
    20	
    21	Using the product from a project (the shim sets `SNAPBACK2_COMPILER`):
    22	`snapback2 init [dir] [--template todos|chat] [--client react|expo|none] [--json]` (Expo requires chat; Quarry `snapback/*.q`),
    23	`snapback2 dev` (prints `SNAPBACK_URL=…`, detaches, attaches if running, `--stop`/`--status`),
    24	`snapback2 check`, `run <op> [args] --as alice|system`, `test`, `why [--as system]`, `trace latest`, `data [--as system]`, `logs`,
    25	`persona list`, `reset`, `guide`.
    26	Jobs: `run <job> [args] --as system [--plan|--wait|--dry-run]`, `run --file repair.q [args] --as system [--plan|--wait|--dry-run]`
    27	(`--dry-run` rehearses on a discarded snapshot: no effects, timers, sweeps, or blob writes),
    28	`run --cancel|--resume <job_id> --as system`, `trace <job_id>`. See `guide job`.
    29	
    30	Gotchas you cannot derive from the code:
    31	- Own Cargo and Bun workspaces; the repo root excludes them. Run cargo/bun from `snapback2/`.
    32	- LLPs for Snapback 2 are numbered **1000+** in the shared `llp/`; `llp-create` must be told.
    33	- No file over 1,500 lines — `caps` (root `node scripts/caps.mjs`) refuses it. Split on the way in.
    34	- The corpus under `corpus/` is shared by the Rust engine, the TS evaluator, and the TS compiler;
    35	  regenerate with `cargo run -p snapback2-core --bin export_corpus`.
    36	- `effects` is off by default; `hermes` needs `IBEX2_VANILLA_HERMES_DIR` + `IBEX2_HERMESC` (see
    37	  `scripts/hermes`). The blocking checks never depend on it; the published binary carries it.
    38	- Backends use `snapback/schema.q`; TypeScript under `snapback/` is reserved for effects, seeds, and journeys.
    39	- The binary needs the bundled compiler worker (`packages/snapback2/dist/compiler.mjs`) for any verb
    40	  that compiles; run `node build.mjs` in `packages/snapback2` after touching the compiler.
    41	- `metrics/loop.jsonl` is append-only and conflicts on every merge; resolve by union.
    42	- Reuse a named lane worktree; never `git stash`; push after `scripts/test.sh` is green.
     1	# Rules
     2	
     3	**Snapback 2 optimizes the edit-to-verified loop — the agent's building an app on it, and ours building it.**
     4	
     5	Speed is the constraint. Everything below exists to keep the edit-to-verified loop short
     6	and to stop this repository accreting apparatus faster than anyone can remove it. These
     7	rules bind `snapback2/` and the LLPs numbered 1000 and up; Snapback 1 is baselined, not
     8	exempt, and its numbers may only fall.
     9	
    10	Each rule is **[check]** (enforced by `caps`) or **[review]** (enforced by a human).
    11	
    12	## Budgets — every budget is a trade, not a limit
    13	
    14	- **5 blocking checks, 60s total.** A sixth requires deleting one, in the same PR. **[check]**
    15	- **59 documents in the working set** (`llp/current/`). Today's count. A 60th requires
    16	  archiving one — which is just removing the link. Snapback 2 aims to leave this at 15;
    17	  Snapback 1's links are archived at promotion. **[check]**
    18	- **12 documents in the foundation** (`llp/foundation/`). **[check]**
    19	- **This file: 700 words.** If it grows, something becomes a check or stops being a rule. **[check]**
    20	- **1,500 lines per source file, baseline 204.** Generated files exempt — and generated
    21	  files are built, not committed; so are benchmark run records under `benchmarks/runs/`,
    22	  which are evidence, not source. The baseline is Snapback 1's oversize count on the day
    23	  the rule landed; it may not grow, and `snapback2/` contributes zero to it. **[check]**
    24	
    25	## Blocking checks
    26	
    27	`caps` · `ref-check` · `snapback2/scripts/test.sh` · `snapback2/scripts/measure.mjs --check` · `check-inventories`
    28	
    29	## Loop shape
    30	
    31	- **Every check reports all failures in one run.** Fail-fast is banned. **[check]**
    32	- **Nothing blocks on anything slower than 60 seconds.** Slow verification runs on a
    33	  schedule, attributed to the breaking commit, and files a bounded set of issues. **[review]**
    34	- **Fix loops get 3 rounds.** Then stop: escalate to a human or descope. **[review]**
    35	
    36	## Time budgets
    37	
    38	Tracked every run of `snapback2/scripts/measure.mjs` (appends `snapback2/metrics/loop.jsonl`).
    39	A regression is a P0 with a name on it.
    40	
    41	| | budget |
    42	|---|---|
    43	| Incremental rebuild after a one-line edit | 15s |
    44	| Test what you just changed | 30s |
    45	| Blocking gate suite | 60s |
    46	| Full build, warm cache | 5 min |
    47	| **`snapback2 check` on a program (the agent's product latency)** | **1s** |
    48	| **`snapback2 doctor` boot-to-ready** | **0.5s** |
    49	
    50	## Scope
    51	
    52	- **`NOT-DOING.md` is binding.** Moving something onto the doing-list means writing why
    53	  and taking something off. **[review]**
    54	- **Delete; don't deprecate.** No compat shims or migration paths before promotion. **[review]**
    55	- **Nothing is compiled, transpiled, or generated at runtime that could be built ahead of
    56	  time.** **[review]**
    57	- **A spec needs an implementer and a date, or it isn't written.** **[review]**
    58	- **Only implemented specs bind.** A document with no running code behind it is a
    59	  proposal, and a proposal cannot block a PR or justify a check. **[review]**
    60	
    61	## Agents
    62	
    63	- **Agents remove apparatus freely and add none.** An agent PR that adds a check, script,
    64	  registry, config file, or design doc needs explicit human approval saying so. **[review]**
    65	- **2026-09-06 session exception:** the user delegated the search implementation
    66	  decisions; one `snapback2-core/build.rs` may generate pinned Unicode tables at
    67	  build time, alongside the existing package build. No new gate, registry,
    68	  runtime generator, or Unicode dependency is authorized by this exception. **[review]**
    69	- **Snapback 2 LLPs are numbered 1000 and up**; `llp-create` must be told. **[review]**
    70	
    71	## Shared machines
    72	
    73	- Warm, named lanes, one per stream of work. Never clone fresh; reuse the worktree.
    74	- **Never `git stash`.** Use a temporary commit.
    75	- Kill only PIDs you recorded at launch. Never by name or pattern.
     1	# What Snapback 2 Does Not Do
     2	
     3	This is the most valuable file in the repository. It is longer than the doing-list on
     4	purpose. Moving something off it means writing one line naming what it unblocks and
     5	taking something off the doing-list in the same change.
     6	
     7	## The bars that make this list derivable
     8	
     9	Three bars, named separately so one name never covers two objects (LLP 1007 §4a;
    10	the author delegated the first two to the orchestrator on 2026-09-02 and added
    11	the third on 2026-09-04, LLP 1012 §12):
    12	
    13	- **The loop bar.** A fresh agent, with only the installed package and the task,
    14	  builds the reduced Clique M1–M3 (accounts, feed, realtime chat; React web; no
    15	  media) on the **published** snapback2 in under 1,200 s with at most one
    16	  Snapback-attributed correction loop, and `snapback2 test` proves it
    17	  (LLP 0329.016 §8). Everything not required by this bar does not exist yet.
    18	- **The capability bar.** `benchmarks/briefs/social-messenger.md` M1–M3 as
    19	  written: Expo on iOS, Android, and web; an avatar; one to four images and one
    20	  video; no external I/O. It starts only after the loop bar is met on a
    21	  published package. When it starts, LLP 1006's first slice is the brief's
    22	  formats **plus HEIC and QuickTime MOV** (author ruling 2026-09-03, held over
    23	  a 2–1 panel recommendation): iPhone cameras produce both, and the brief's
    24	  own real-phone acceptance cannot pass without them. The offsetting item is
    25	  non-media `file` columns, which LLP 1006 §13a designs and defers to a later
    26	  slice. Until then the media and Expo slice stays on this list.
    27	- **The offline bar.** `benchmarks/briefs/social-messenger.md` M5 acceptance 5,
    28	  M6 acceptance 1 and 2, LLP 1012 §10's physical process-kill witness, and
    29	  one acceptance only the client twin can pass (a never-opened chat renders
    30	  offline through its query; an offline send is predicted into that thread),
    31	  green on the Expo iOS simulator and React web on a **published** package
    32	  (author ruling 2026-09-04, LLP 1012 §12; the twin acceptance from the
    33	  author's principle the same day: go straight to the ideal, no staging). It starts when the loop bar is met
    34	  and runs alongside the capability bar's Expo arm, which shares the carrier.
    35	  Until then local-first v1 is specified, not built.
    36	
    37	## Not building
    38	
    39	- **A second authoring profile, a plan algebra, or a JS handler lane.** One language —
    40	  Quarry (LLP 1004; the TypeScript authoring surface was deleted on 2026-09-02, LLP
    41	  1007 §4b) — one spelling. Quarry binds directly to typed IR. TypeScript is limited to
    42	  seeds, journeys, and effect bodies, never a lowering target or a way to author tables,
    43	  rules, queries, or mutations. Familiar vs Explicit cost every v1 doc twice (LLP 0317
    44	  bundle 6).
    45	- **JavaScript in the data plane.** Queries and mutations run in the Rust IR
    46	  interpreter inside the SQLite transaction. Ibex 2 is linked only by
    47	  `snapback2-effects`, only with `--features hermes`, only for effects.
    48	- **Committed generated code.** Types by inference; IR, certificate, and stubs are a
    49	  gitignored build cache. No `generated/`, no review manifest, no digest-named imports.
    50	- **An auth *mode*.** Authentication is always on; development has personas (`--as`).
    51	- **A supervised bundler.** The server never spawns, proxies, or watches Metro or Vite.
    52	  The client contract is `SNAPBACK_URL`.
    53	- **Exact 2 until Unicode prefix search qualifies on React web and Expo**, and
    54	  client arms beyond those three. The 2026-09-06 session-delegated move below
    55	  postpones Exact 2 to fund coherent indexed online/offline search. No Swift,
    56	  Kotlin, or Dart.
    57	- **Media collections, push, cron, vector/ranked search, hosted control planes,
    58	  multi-region, federation.** Exact Unicode word-prefix indexing is the narrow
    59	  2026-09-06 search exception below. Assets (typed intent), one `channel` kind, one `schedule` kind, one
    60	  hosted target: that is the feature list until the bar is met. Exception (author ruling 2026-09-05, LLP 1015 §7, §8.7): lane H0 of the
    61	  control plane — `deploy` to a directory, the release ledger, snapshots, `rollback`,
    62	  `secret`, and token admission in `serve` mode; no provider, no registry — may start
    63	  before the loop bar. The hosted lanes H1–H3 still wait for it and for LLP 1015 §11.
    64	- **Offline beyond local-first v1** (LLP 1012, author rulings 2026-09-04). v1 is the
